metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-lines-11 = cons "## Univalent: equivalence is identity" (cons "" (cons "Vladimir Voevodsky's univalence axiom (2009) says that for types, equivalence is equality: if there is an equivalence between two things, they are the same, and every construction on one transports along the equivalence to the other. Cubical type theory made univalence compute, so transport is evaluation." (cons "" (cons "Applied to the unison, a statebox has many hashes, one per presentation, and the paths between them are kept in the store as data. Refactoring a process is proving a path: rewrite a settlement flow for speed, prove its runs equivalent to the old flow's once, and every proof, bound and dependent built on the old flow holds for the new one by transport. Hosting identifies equivalent processes as one. Part I carries a unit exactly when the unit is declared equivalent to blocks of it: the book itself is held up to equivalence. Meaning in language is transport: a word's present occurrence is identified with past occurrences, and what followed them is carried to now." (cons "" (cons "This is what Leibniz asked for. His characteristica universalis wanted every concept given an exact character and every dispute settled by calculation, and it called for identity without primitives, a calculus of change, and the language people actually speak. Univalence gives identity as a path, the cube gives change, and the record of natural language is the medium itself. Unison supplies exact characters that no registry owns. Calculemus becomes normalization with witnesses." (cons "" nil)))))))
